In Apache Airflow, tasks execute in completely isolated environments, often on entirely different physical worker nodes. 
To bridge this gap, Airflow uses a messaging system called XComs—short for Cross-Communications—but misunderstanding 
what XComs are actually built for is the number one cause of total Airflow outages.

Technical Breakdown :- 
By design, Airflow tasks do not share memory. If Task A needs to send information to Task B, 
it performs an xcom_push. This serializes a small key-value pair and physically writes it into Airflow’s 
underlying metadata database (usually Postgres or MySQL). Later, Task B performs an xcom_pull to query that 
database and retrieve the value. In modern Airflow using the TaskFlow API (the @task decorator), 
this is handled invisibly for you: returning a value from a Python function automatically pushes it to XCom, 
and passing it as an argument to the next function automatically pulls it.

Buisness Context :- 
XComs are essential for dynamic, cloud-native orchestration. For example, if your pipeline dynamically spins up
an ephemeral AWS EMR cluster to save money, the exact cluster_id isn't known ahead of time. 
Task A spins it up and pushes the generated cluster_id to XCom. 
Task B pulls that exact ID to submit the Spark job, and Task C pulls it to shut the cluster down, 
ensuring we don't leave expensive ghost infrastructure running.

Design Decision :-
The most lethal anti-pattern I see from some engineers is attempting to pass actual data—like a 50MB Pandas 
DataFrame—through an XCom. Because XComs are stored directly in the orchestrator's Postgres database,
this instantly bloats the tables, consumes all connections, and crashes the entire Airflow instance 
for the whole company. As a data engineer, I enforce the 'Pointer Pattern.' Task A writes the 50MB
file to an S3 bucket, and pushes only the S3 URI string (a few bytes) to XCom. 
Task B pulls the string and downloads the payload directly from S3, keeping Airflow's database completely 
isolated from the data plane.

